    "problem_statement": "# Issues with to_dict() method in addict.Dict\n\n## Description\n\nI'm experiencing issues with the `to_dict()` method in addict. It's not correctly converting nested structures and is modifying string values.\n\n## To Reproduce\n\nHere's a simple example that demonstrates the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom addict import Dict\n\n# Create a Dict with nested structures\nd = Dict()\nd.a = [Dict(a=1), Dict(b=2), \"test\"]\nd.b = Dict(c=3)\nd.c = \"hello\"\n\n# Convert to regular dict\nregular_dict = d.to_dict()\n\nprint(regular_dict)\n# Expected: {'a': [{'a': 1}, {'b': 2}, 'test'], 'b': {'c': 3}, 'c': 'hello'}\n# Actual: {'a': [{'a': 1}, {'b': 2}], 'b': {'c': 3}, 'c': 5}\n```\n\nThe issues are:\n1. Non-Dict items in lists are being filtered out (the string \"test\" is missing)\n2. String values are being replaced with their length (the string \"hello\" becomes 5)\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe `to_dict()` method should:\n1. Preserve all items in lists/tuples, not just Dict objects\n2. Keep string values as they are, not convert them to their length\n\n## Environment Info\n- Python version: 3.10\n- addict version: latest"
